EXTRA['50-30-20-budgeting-guide'] = '''<h2>Common mistakes with the 50/30/20 rule</h2>
<p>Most people who abandon the rule do so for one of three reasons. The first is treating the percentages as a law rather than a starting point. If your rent alone takes 45% of your take-home pay, forcing everything else into the remaining 5% of "needs" is unrealistic. Adjust the split, then work to move it back over time.</p>
<p>The second mistake is hiding needs inside the wants bucket, or the reverse. A basic phone plan is a need; the premium unlimited plan with a new handset every year is mostly a want. Being honest about this is the whole exercise. The third mistake is skipping the review. Prices change, so a ten-minute check once a month keeps the budget accurate and stops small leaks from growing.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<p><b>Should I use gross or net income?</b> Use net income, the amount that actually arrives in your account after tax and payroll deductions. If your employer takes retirement contributions before pay, count those as part of your savings share.</p>
<p><b>Do debt payments count as needs or savings?</b> Minimum payments are needs because missing them has real consequences. Any extra amount you send to debt belongs in the 20% savings and debt bucket.</p>
<p><b>What if my income changes every month?</b> Base your budget on your lowest typical month and treat anything above it as a bonus that goes straight to savings or debt. The <a href="/tools/50-30-20-budget-calculator/">50/30/20 budget calculator</a> lets you test different incomes in seconds.</p>'''

EXTRA['compound-interest-wealth-building'] = '''<h2>Worked examples you can check yourself</h2>
<p>Saving $200 a month at an assumed 7% annual return grows to roughly $34,600 after 10 years, about $244,000 after 30 years and about $525,000 after 40 years. In the 30-year case you contributed only $72,000 yourself. Doubling the monthly amount to $400 doubles the result to around $488,000, which shows that the growth is proportional to what you put in, while the length of time changes the result far more than the size of each deposit.</p>
<h2>How often interest compounds, and why fees matter</h2>
<p>Interest can compound daily, monthly or yearly. More frequent compounding helps a little, but the difference between monthly and daily is small compared with the effect of the rate itself. Fees work the same way in reverse: an investment fund charging 1% a year takes a bite out of your balance every year, and that lost amount can no longer compound for you. Over 30 years a 1% annual fee can reduce your final balance by a quarter or more, so compare costs before choosing a product.</p>
<h2>Common mistakes</h2>
<ul><li><b>Waiting for a perfect time to start.</b> Small early deposits beat large late ones because they have more time to compound.</li><li><b>Using unrealistic return assumptions.</b> Long-term averages are not promises. Test several rates, such as 4%, 6% and 8%, in the <a href="/tools/compound-interest-calculator/">compound interest calculator</a>.</li><li><b>Ignoring inflation.</b> A balance that looks large in 30 years will buy less than the same number does today.</li><li><b>Carrying high-interest debt while investing.</b> Compounding works against you on a credit card, often at a rate higher than you can reliably earn.</li></ul>'''

EXTRA['side-hustles-for-financial-freedom'] = '''<h2>Taxes, records and getting paid</h2>
<p>Side income is usually taxable, and tracking it from day one saves stress later. Keep a simple record of every payment received and every business expense, and set money aside from each payment so a tax bill never surprises you. Many freelancers reserve roughly a quarter to a third of their earnings, but the right amount depends on your country, your other income and the deductions you qualify for. Check your local tax authority's guidance or speak to a qualified accountant.</p>
<h2>Mistakes that slow people down</h2>
<ul><li><b>Starting three hustles at once.</b> Each one needs a ramp-up period. Focus on a single option long enough to judge it fairly.</li><li><b>Underpricing out of fear.</b> Work out the hourly rate you need after costs and fees, and use it as your floor.</li><li><b>Spending the income as it arrives.</b> Decide in advance where side earnings go, for example 70% to savings or debt and 30% to enjoy.</li><li><b>Ignoring your main job's rules.</b> Check your employment contract for conflict-of-interest or outside-work clauses.</li></ul>'''

EXTRA['how-to-save-first-1000-dollars'] = '''<h2>Seven ways to find the first $50 a week</h2>
<ul><li><b>Audit recurring charges.</b> Cancelling two unused subscriptions often frees $20 or more a month immediately. See our list of <a href="/articles/unnecessary-subscriptions-to-cancel/">subscriptions to cancel</a>.</li><li><b>Lower your grocery bill.</b> Meal planning and store brands can free $10 to $25 a week. Our <a href="/articles/smart-grocery-shopping-hacks/">grocery guide</a> shows how.</li><li><b>Sell what you no longer use.</b> Electronics, furniture and clothing can turn into a one-off boost.</li><li><b>Pause new purchases for 48 hours.</b> Many wants fade within two days.</li><li><b>Add a small side income.</b> Even a few hours a week of tutoring or freelancing speeds things up.</li><li><b>Send windfalls to savings.</b> Tax refunds, gifts and bonuses can cover weeks of the plan at once.</li><li><b>Round up purchases.</b> If your bank offers it, round-up transfers add savings with no effort.</li></ul>
<h2>Mistakes to avoid</h2>
<p>The most common mistake is keeping the money in your everyday account, where it is easy to spend. Open a separate account and name it for the goal. The second mistake is quitting after a bad week. If an unexpected bill takes some of your progress, restart the plan with the new balance rather than abandoning it.</p>
<h2>What to do once you reach $1,000</h2>
<p>Keep the habit going. Move the same weekly amount toward a full emergency fund of three to six months of essential costs, or toward high-interest debt if you have any. Read our guide to <a href="/articles/beginner-guide-to-emergency-funds/">emergency funds</a> to choose your next target, and use the <a href="/tools/compound-interest-calculator/">compound interest calculator</a> to see what the habit becomes over years.</p>'''

NEW['unnecessary-subscriptions-to-cancel'] = '''<p>Subscriptions are designed to be forgotten. A few dollars here and there never feels painful, yet a typical household can easily pay for a dozen recurring services. At $10 each that is $120 a month, or $1,440 a year, for things you may barely use. The good news is that an honest audit takes about half an hour and can pay for itself the same day.</p>
<h2>Seven subscriptions worth checking first</h2>
<ol><li><b>Streaming services.</b> Most people use one or two regularly. Keep the one you watch most and rotate others in for a month at a time when a show you want arrives.</li><li><b>Gym memberships.</b> If you have not visited in the last two months, the real cost per visit is probably very high. Cancel, or switch to a pay-as-you-go option, and use free alternatives such as walking, running or bodyweight workouts.</li><li><b>Extra cloud storage.</b> Check what you are storing. Deleting duplicate photos and old backups can bring you back under the free tier.</li><li><b>Duplicate apps.</b> Two music services, two note-taking apps or two VPNs is one too many. Pick one and cancel the other.</li><li><b>News and magazine plans.</b> Many local libraries give free digital access to major newspapers and magazines.</li><li><b>Meal kits.</b> If boxes pile up unused or you skip deliveries, the per-meal cost is not worth it.</li><li><b>Free trials that converted.</b> Trials that ask for a card are built to roll into a paid plan. Check your statement for charges you do not recognise.</li></ol>
<h2>How to run a 30-minute audit</h2>
<p>Download the last two or three months of statements from your bank and card accounts. Highlight every charge that repeats, then list each one with its monthly cost. Next to each, write the last date you used it. If you cannot remember, that is a strong sign it can go. Cancel what you do not use, then decide whether the remaining ones are worth their price.</p>
<p>Annual charges are easy to miss, so look back twelve months where you can. A yearly software renewal or domain fee that appears once will not show in a short review.</p>
<h2>Cancelling without the runaround</h2>
<ul><li>Look for the cancel option in account settings first. Many services let you cancel online.</li><li>If a company makes cancelling difficult, take screenshots of each step and keep any confirmation email.</li><li>Check whether you are in a contract period or whether cancelling takes effect at the end of the billing cycle.</li><li>If a charge continues after cancelling, contact your bank or card issuer. In many countries you can dispute charges you did not authorise.</li></ul>
<h2>Prevent subscription creep</h2>
<p>Use a separate card or a calendar reminder for free trials, so you cancel before the first charge. Add a quarterly reminder to repeat the audit. Before signing up for anything new, ask whether it replaces something you already pay for.</p>
<h2>What to do with the savings</h2>
<p>Cancelling $60 a month frees $720 a year, but only if the money goes somewhere useful. Move the amount to savings on the same day as the cancellation takes effect. If you are starting from zero, that money can build your first $1,000 in under two years, and much sooner alongside other changes. See our guide to <a href="/articles/how-to-save-first-1000-dollars/">saving your first $1,000</a>, or plan the bigger picture with the <a href="/tools/50-30-20-budget-calculator/">50/30/20 budget calculator</a>, where subscriptions belong in the "wants" bucket.</p>'''

NEW['beginner-guide-to-emergency-funds'] = '''<p>An emergency fund is money set aside for surprises you cannot predict but can be fairly sure will happen: a car repair, a medical bill, a broken laptop you need for work, or a gap between jobs. Without it, those events end up on a credit card or a high-interest loan. With it, they become an annoying afternoon instead of a financial setback.</p>
<h2>What counts as an emergency</h2>
<p>A real emergency is unexpected, necessary and urgent. A job loss, an urgent home repair or an unplanned medical expense qualifies. A holiday, a sale or a new phone because yours is a year old does not. Keeping the definition tight protects the fund, so it is still there when you need it.</p>
<h2>How much do you really need?</h2>
<ul><li><b>Starter fund: about $1,000, or one month of essentials.</b> This covers most small shocks and is the first milestone.</li><li><b>Standard fund: three to six months of essential expenses.</b> Essentials are the things you must pay even if your income stops: housing, food, utilities, transport, insurance and minimum debt payments.</li><li><b>Larger cushion: six to nine months or more.</b> This suits people with irregular income, a single household income, dependants or specialised jobs that take longer to replace.</li></ul>
<p>To find your number, add up your monthly essentials and multiply by the months you chose. If essentials cost $2,400 a month, three months is $7,200 and six months is $14,400. Those totals look large, which is why the starter fund comes first.</p>
<h2>Where to keep it</h2>
<p>The ideal home is safe, easy to reach and separate from your everyday account. A high-yield savings account fits well because your money earns interest and stays accessible. Avoid locking it into investments that can drop in value or accounts with withdrawal penalties. Our guide to <a href="/articles/high-yield-savings-accounts-explained/">high-yield savings accounts</a> explains what to compare.</p>
<h2>How to build it step by step</h2>
<ol><li>Open a separate savings account and give it a clear name.</li><li>Set a first target of $1,000 and a date.</li><li>Automate a transfer for payday, even if the amount is small.</li><li>Add windfalls such as tax refunds and gifts.</li><li>Once you reach the starter amount, raise the target toward one month, then three months of essentials.</li></ol>
<h2>Emergency fund or debt first?</h2>
<p>A common approach is to build the starter fund first, then attack high-interest debt, then grow the fund toward the full size. The starter fund stops new emergencies from adding to the debt. Once credit card balances are gone, you can put more weight on the larger cushion. The <a href="/tools/debt-payoff-calculator/">debt payoff planner</a> helps you see how fast you can clear what you owe.</p>
<h2>Using and replacing the fund</h2>
<p>When you use the fund, do not feel guilty. That is what it is for. Then make a plan to refill it, even if you pause other savings goals for a short time. Treat it like a bill you pay to yourself until it is back to target.</p>
<h2>Common mistakes</h2>
<ul><li>Keeping the money in a current account where it gets spent.</li><li>Raiding it for planned expenses; use a separate sinking fund for those instead.</li><li>Waiting until you can save the whole amount before starting.</li><li>Forgetting to adjust the target when your costs go up.</li></ul>
<p>An emergency fund is not exciting, but it is one of the most reliable ways to protect your finances and your peace of mind.</p>'''

NEW['frugal-living-tips-without-deprivation'] = '''<p>Frugal living is often confused with going without. A better description is spending deliberately: paying full price for the things that matter to you and cutting what does not. Done well, it feels like control rather than sacrifice. The ten habits below are practical, easy to start and add up to meaningful monthly savings.</p>
<h2>Ten habits that save money without misery</h2>
<ol><li><b>Plan meals weekly.</b> Food waste is expensive. A simple plan and shopping list cut impulse purchases and unused ingredients. Our <a href="/articles/smart-grocery-shopping-hacks/">grocery guide</a> has more detail.</li><li><b>Choose store brands.</b> For staples such as flour, pasta, rice and tinned goods, the quality gap is often small and the price gap large.</li><li><b>Wait 48 hours on non-essential purchases.</b> If you still want it after two days, buy it with a clear conscience.</li><li><b>Borrow before you buy.</b> Libraries lend books, audiobooks, films and often tools, games and museum passes.</li><li><b>Review insurance and phone plans yearly.</b> Providers often reward new customers more than loyal ones. Compare offers and call your current provider with the best quote in hand.</li><li><b>Reduce energy use.</b> LED bulbs, smart plugs for standby devices and sealing draughts lower bills with very little effort.</li><li><b>Buy secondhand.</b> Furniture, clothing, tools and sports gear can often be found in good condition for much less.</li><li><b>Cancel what you do not use.</b> Start with the <a href="/articles/unnecessary-subscriptions-to-cancel/">subscription audit</a>.</li><li><b>Cook in batches.</b> Making a large pot once and freezing portions saves money and time on busy days.</li><li><b>Use cashback tools carefully.</b> They help if you were going to buy anyway. They hurt if they make you buy more.</li></ol>
<h2>How to start without burning out</h2>
<p>Do not try all ten at once. Choose three that fit your life and track the savings for a month. For example, planning meals might save $30 a week, cancelling two subscriptions might save $25 a month, and a yearly insurance review might save $200 or more. Small wins build the habit and make the next step easier.</p>
<h2>Know your value-based spending</h2>
<p>A helpful exercise is to list the five things you would be most unhappy to give up: perhaps coffee with friends, a hobby, travel or a particular streaming service. Protect those in your budget on purpose. Then look hard at everything else. This turns frugality from a rule you resent into a choice you made.</p>
<h2>Avoid false economies</h2>
<ul><li>Buying the cheapest version of something you use daily and replacing it twice.</li><li>Driving across town for a small discount that costs more in fuel and time.</li><li>Stockpiling bulk offers you will not finish before they expire.</li><li>Skipping essential maintenance, such as servicing a car, which can lead to far larger bills.</li></ul>
<h2>Where the savings should go</h2>
<p>Savings only help if they are directed. Automate a transfer for the amount you save so it does not leak back into spending. Early on, use it for a starter emergency fund; later, for debt or investing. Saving $30 a week adds up to over $1,500 a year, and with the <a href="/tools/compound-interest-calculator/">compound interest calculator</a> you can see how regular amounts grow over time.</p>
<p>Frugality works best when it supports a goal you care about. Know what you are saving for, and the small choices get much easier.</p>'''

NEW['how-to-stop-impulse-buying'] = '''<p>Impulse buying is not a character flaw. Retailers spend huge sums studying how to make buying feel effortless: one-click checkout, countdown timers, "only three left" banners, personalised ads and endless social feeds. Understanding why it happens and having a few simple rules in place is far more effective than relying on willpower.</p>
<h2>Why impulse purchases happen</h2>
<p>Buying something new triggers a short burst of anticipation and reward. Stress, boredom, tiredness and loneliness make the urge stronger, because a purchase feels like a quick fix for an uncomfortable mood. Marketing then removes the friction between the urge and the checkout. The result is a purchase made in seconds that you may regret for weeks.</p>
<h2>Common triggers to watch for</h2>
<ul><li><b>Scarcity and urgency.</b> Limited-time offers push you to decide before you have thought it through.</li><li><b>Saved payment details.</b> The fewer steps between you and the purchase, the easier it is to buy.</li><li><b>Social media.</b> Influencers and targeted ads show you things you did not know you wanted.</li><li><b>Emotional states.</b> Shopping after a hard day, or when celebrating, often leads to spending beyond your plan.</li><li><b>Sales.</b> A 40% discount on something you did not need is still a 100% cost.</li></ul>
<h2>Seven rules that work</h2>
<ol><li><b>Wait 48 hours.</b> Put the item in a note or wish list, and decide later. Many urges fade without any effort.</li><li><b>Remove saved cards.</b> A few extra seconds of typing details gives your rational side time to catch up.</li><li><b>Unsubscribe from promotional emails.</b> If you do not see the offer, you cannot be tempted by it. Turn off shopping notifications too.</li><li><b>Keep a wish list.</b> Review it monthly. Items you still want after a month are better candidates for purchase.</li><li><b>Set a guilt-free fun budget.</b> A small monthly amount for spontaneous purchases stops the all-or-nothing cycle. When it is gone, it is gone.</li><li><b>Use the cost-per-use test.</b> Divide the price by the number of times you will realistically use the item.</li><li><b>Ask one question.</b> Will I still use this in three months?</li></ol>
<h2>Translate prices into time</h2>
<p>Convert the price into hours of work. If you earn $20 an hour after tax, a $120 jacket costs six hours of your life. That reframing often makes the decision easier. You can also compare the purchase with a goal: $120 is more than two weeks of the plan to save your first $1,000.</p>
<h2>Build a better environment</h2>
<p>Willpower is limited, so design your surroundings. Shop with a list, avoid browsing as entertainment, and take a break from shopping apps when you are stressed. If you tend to overspend online, leave your card out of the browser's autofill. If you overspend on food delivery, delete the app for a month and see what happens.</p>
<h2>When you slip</h2>
<p>Everyone makes impulse purchases. Check the return policy, return what you can, and note which trigger caught you so you can plan for it next time. Do not abandon your budget because of one purchase. Use the <a href="/tools/50-30-20-budget-calculator/">50/30/20 budget calculator</a> to see how much room your wants category really has, and put your savings on autopilot, as described in our <a href="/articles/financial-habits-of-successful-people/">guide to daily financial habits</a>.</p>
<p>If impulse spending feels compulsive, causes distress or is tied to debt you cannot manage, speak to a counsellor or a free non-profit money advice service in your country.</p>'''

